## Why these and not others

A sweep of 366 Visual Basic 6 repositories found 120 that hold both source and
an executable.
Only 52 of those carry a licence that permits redistribution.
`.planning/research/CORPUS.md` records the measurement, and names what was
rejected and why.

Microsoft's own VB98 samples are not here.
The Visual Studio 6 licence grants no redistribution right, and the samples
shipped without executables, so they carry no ground truth.

## A known defect in one corpus file

`corpus/vb6-code/Hidden-Markov-model/frmHMM.frx` is damaged, and it arrived
that way.

The file holds one record, the `Text` property of a text box. Its record header
declares 56 bytes of payload. Only 55 are present. The file ends with a lone
`0x0A` where a `0x0D 0x0A` pair belongs, so one carriage return was removed by
end of line normalisation.

The damage is upstream, not in this copy. This copy is byte for byte identical
to the upstream clone, and `core.autocrlf` was unset on the machine that cloned
it, so no normalisation happened here. Upstream marks `.frx` as `-diff`, which
suppresses the diff but does not stop normalisation.

**The bytes are kept as upstream holds them.** Repairing the file would invent
data and would make this copy no longer a faithful record of its origin. The
differential gate excludes this one property, names this note as the reason,
and must not silently pass it.

`.gitattributes` in this repository marks `.frx` as binary so that the same
loss cannot happen here.
